      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The 4-second hold creates a sustained hypoxia (low oxygen) response that triggers a chemical cascade known as the baroreflex. During this pause, your brainstem releases norepinephrine to maintain respiration, which literally \"refreshes\" and recalibrates your autonomic nervous system's baseline. This specific timing is crucial because it provides enough duration to override the reflexive breathing patterns of the adrenal system without causing the panic associated with hyperventilation during a long hold.",
